diff --git a/deploy/aws/EXECUTOR.md b/deploy/aws/EXECUTOR.md
index ad7910c7b8c0286eeb104979f167e9add9c92d0e..2af5c950af4530997cdfb7b0480523a985eaab49 100644
--- a/deploy/aws/EXECUTOR.md
+++ b/deploy/aws/EXECUTOR.md
@@ -66,3 +66,64 @@ Secrets Manager, rotate credentials or apply an AWS deployment automatically.
 Deployment is incomplete until those external steps and acceptance checks pass.
 The local Firecracker scaffold remains unsupported; selecting it does not satisfy
 this executor contract.
+
+## Managed credential refresh (deployment opt-in)
+
+The AWS operator may set `executor_managed_credentials = true` after qualifying
+the separate token publisher. This deployment option is disabled by default.
+The local installer keeps its existing inline kubeconfig path; it must not offer
+customer-editable access to production secret ARNs or controller identities.
+This is credential maintenance for an existing backend, not a new user-selected
+sandbox. Existing inline-token and inline-certificate deployments are unchanged.
+
+The managed contract is intentionally restricted to the reviewed Ohio production
+deployment: account `589997396906`, region `us-east-2`, namespace
+`lightwork-execution`, service account `lightwork-controller`, and the exact
+Secrets Manager ARN for `lightwork-prod/executor-kubeconfig`. Terraform exposes
+that ARN as `MAVERICK_EXECUTOR_KUBECONFIG_SECRET_ARN` and grants the application
+task role only `GetSecretValue` for that ARN with `VersionStage=AWSCURRENT`.
+The existing task execution role still injects
+`MAVERICK_EXECUTOR_KUBECONFIG_SECRET` at startup. That injected JSON remains an
+immutable cluster/service-account identity pin, even after its initial token
+expires. The reader never rewrites that environment value or its old file.
+
+The publisher must supply exactly one flattened JSON context with an inline
+TokenRequest bearer token, matching cluster server, CA, TLS settings, context,
+namespace, controller service-account UID, issuer and audience. No kubeconfig
+extensions, command plugins, proxy, local file references or alternate contexts
+are accepted. Tokens must bind to one of
+`lightwork-prod-controller-binding-a`, `-b` or `-c` with a well-formed object UID.
+The Kubernetes API verifies signatures and object binding; local JWT decoding
+alone is not proof of authentication. The publisher must leave an earlier bound
+Secret intact until all tokens issued against it have expired, and must never
+delete/revoke a token while a command may still need its export/recovery window.
+
+Before every remote kubectl operation, the application checks token lifetime
+against the operation timeout plus 60 seconds. Before starting a command it also
+reserves the export timeout. It checks AWSCURRENT at least once per 30 seconds
+of active use, using the standard verified AWS HTTPS endpoint, two-second
+connect and three-second read timeouts, and one total SDK attempt. Successful
+refresh does not restart ECS or interrupt an existing kubectl stream.
+
+Identity drift, malformed tokens, an older credential version, insufficient
+lifetime or AWS refresh failure pauses new operations without falling back to
+the original injected token or a stale cached credential. Error messages do not
+include token contents or AWS response bodies. A private atomic credential file
+is separate from the original inline file. A private, nonsecret high-water
+record prevents version rollback across backend reconstruction and process
+restart. The record is part of protected control-plane state: restoring an old
+data root must not restore older authority without reconciliation.
+
+The process cache has a thread lock and the files use the existing strict
+cross-process lock. This does not establish distributed exclusion on EFS or
+replace the production singleton/all-writer controls. NFS behavior and actual
+service-account claims must be qualified on the target before activation.
+
+Required additional acceptance: issue two real bound-token versions, verify
+active commands continue while later calls use the replacement without ECS
+restart, reject rollback/identity changes, simulate refresh failure and expired
+tokens, verify a failed export remains recoverable, and check worker-side
+denials. Initial configuration and issuer/CA/service-account changes remain
+separately reviewed deployment changes. This module neither creates nor runs
+the publisher, issues tokens, modifies their RBAC or qualifies their rotation
+schedule automatically.
